nd stiffness to the duration that  it takes them to do these five times six  stand test and hope that they can  actually pull out biomarkers from this  actigraph linked device that would allow  them to basically assess that without  having to get all of this other data so  here's a very challenging graph to read  but in essence what you're seeing here  on the y-axis are those patient reported  scores so the higher the score the more  pain and stiffness the patient reports  and then on the bottom is the normalized  sit-to-stand  test duration so this is the amount of  time that it takes them to do the test  and what you can see is that in general  the more pain that's read out or pain  and stiffness that's reported you see a  longer duration in actually being able  to do this test and so in this paper the  author's talk about the ability to  actually distinguish morning and evening  stiffness and more importantly that  morning stiffness is more severe as  patients report both in the 5 5 times  sit to stand test as well as in what  they can pull out of this actigraph  monitor as well so how does this relate  to rheumatoid arthritis models in the  animals so in this case what I'll show  you here is data from a collagen induced  arthritis model so here we're using a  very standard model in which we give  animals collagen to induce arthritis in  the hind paws of the animals it's one of  the most commonly used models in  rheumatoid arthritis research it nicely  recapitulates disease symptoms and other  features of the disease much like in the  Coopers M model we have an AK LeMay ssin  time period then we induce the animals  through a number  of these injections and then we'll  monitor them over time and and that is  what I show here is the treatment phase  traditionally in a study like this you  would measure a number of different  things you would look at joint thickness  so I'll show you in just a second but  you take calipers and measure the the  ankle size of the animal you might do a  subjective scoring of our thright as'  and you would take those pause and look  at histology and the animals over time  so again while each of these are  important  none of them are directly applicable to  the common kind of outcome measures that  are looked at in the humans in the form  of you know reporting out on pain and  stiffness and whatnot so just looking  quickly at arthritis scores and  thickness measurements so here your  standard assay parameters you would ask  a research associate to go in and to  actually look at the paws of the animals  it's a bit of a small picture but  depending upon how many toes appear to  be inflamed and whether the inflammation  has actually gone up the foot you would  give the animal a different score and  then you would take that same animal and  measure the joint thickness using a pair  of calipers and you can see but that  looks like on the right challenge with  both of these is that they're fairly  subjective and in terms of caliper  measurements one technician may squeeze  a different amount versus another  technician so what I wanted to do is  quickly just show you some data that we  gathered from our own lab so here you  can see joint size measured across the  course of this 17 day study and I'm  showing you two control groups in the  green and the light blue so you can see  really no change in joint thickness  across the full 17 days and then I have  two groups on a group in a b group both  these received collagen but my question  to the audience here is which one  received the more effective drug give  you in one second and then show you that  actually both of them are vehicle  animals so what I really wanted to just  point out here is that research  associate number one took the joint  thickness measurements from the first  set of animals and research associate  number two took joint thickness  measurements on a second set of animals  in a separate study so this just points  out how challenging it is to actually  reproduce across studies and potentially  across technicians trained exactly in  the same way together on the same  measurements in our lab so what we  wanted to do was create an algorithm to  identify the disease without having to  rely on these subjective measurements so  what I'm just showing you on this slide  is a kind of broad strokes overview of  how we actually did that we took from  more than I believe a hundred and fifty  different animals those joint size  measurements as well as the arthritis  scores and histology measurements we  took all of those together as our  expected results for each individual  animal and then we took the the  corresponding motion data and we  extracted a number of different features  from it for example how active was the  animal during the night time how active  were they during the day how fast were  they as examples and we look to see  which of those features best predicts  the expected results so in essence what  we discovered is that the maximum speed  at night actually is the best predictor  of some of the standard measures that  you're looking at histology being a  really important one  arthritis score and and joint  measurements so in essence what we're  showing here is how or what we're saying  is that the the more the arthritis the  animal has the less quickly they can  move  around the cage at night which  remembering back to those patients in  the clinic seems to be a very direct  measure now we're talking about in that  case we were talking about how quickly  could they complete a sit to stand a  test here we're talking about how  quickly they can move around the cage so  on this slide I'm just showing you again  the data you'll notice the joint size  and the arthritis score is identical  from what you saw on this a couple of  slides ago and overlaid on that now in  blue are the digital arthritis index  that we created using this automated  algorithm and I think what's most  telling here is that when 