pathology score and the  digital arthritis index that would  suggest that there was no statistically  significant improvement  so here the this digital measure of  monitoring arthritis is better at  predicting histopathology than some of  those standard measures in life and the  animal the last one is MC 111 which is  actually quite interesting because  there's no real improvement across any  of the measures histopathology  inflammation arthritis or joint size but  we actually see an improvement in the  digital arthritis index and what's  interesting about this compound is it's  actually a corticosteroid so this one  might actually make sense as well in  this case we're probably relieving the  pain and so we're seeing a fun event  that appears to be functional even  though it's not pathologically improving  so what's what's valuable in this case  about being able to use something like a  digital biomarker  is you can see not only changes in kind  of pathology and overall changes in in  joints in this case but you can actually  see a functional improvement I'm so  taken together we get far more better  interesting data when we take digital  biomarkers and we compare them to  standard biomarkers so just quickly to  go through this this model it's possible  for us to create algorithms to predict  the disease states again between the rat  and the patient with rheumatoid  arthritis we see a similar feature  appears to be very important which is  kind of speed of action the digital  biomarkers are more consistent and  reliable than some of the subjective  scoring techniques and that it allows us  to assess both the functional  consequence and the functional response  so is there an improvement in this case  a pain does the pain reliever have an  improvement even if you don't see an  actual functional improvement so with  that I want to finish by talking about  five key opportunities for digital  biomarkers five kind of things that we  still need to improve on to improve t