t of  work and or there is a lot of work  ongoing and how to validate digital  biomarkers in clinic but we should be  thinking more about broadening that back  into the preclinical phase as well to  really be able to do this effectively we  need to think about collecting digital  biomarkers at scale as well so in our  lab we've collected over a billion hours  of digital metric data on many different  strains of animals my  rats and mice many many different  disease models what we've learned in the  clinic that we need to take back to  preclinical in the clinic the ability to  create these predictive measures has  really only come as we're collecting  more and more of this data from a wider  swath of the population and so what that  really suggests is that the more data we  can collect on our animals in the  preclinical phase the more likely we are  to be able to create these better models  and to be able to translate them or to  understand what is translatable or not  translatable into the clinic and then  the last is really building platforms or  creating platforms that allow us to  build better algorithms through AI so  today I talked a bit about some of the  digital biomarkers associated with  behavioral data and the Cooper's own  model we talked a bit about or we didn't  talk today at all about physiologic data  but we've got data in respiratory and  respiratory function as well I showed  you some data from histology we talked  some about manual observations it's  really not until we can collate and  collect all of that data into one easy  platform that we're going to be able to  create some of the best models through  AI and right now if you look at most  labs manual observations like taking  body weights on a scale or collected  through Excel spreadsheets histology may  live in a completely different program  the digital biomarker data of course or  molecular biomarkers we really need to  be able to integrate all those together  and really and I put it in a se